A CSS outline lists every top-level rule: a style rule by its selectors, an at-rule by its keyword and prelude, each on one line.
- A selector list or media query wrapped over several lines prints on one line with its numbers, `>` and `>=` included, and strings stay as written.
- The rules inside a top-level at-rule's block (`@media`, `@supports`, `@scope`) are listed one level deeper; the rules nested in a style rule (`&:hover`) and `@keyframes` steps, in any case, aren't.
- `@charset` and stray CSS declarations aren't rules worth listing.
== args
input/theme.css
== file input/theme.css
@charset "UTF-8";
@import url("reset.css") screen;
@layer base, components;

:root {
  --brand: #333;
}

.button,
.nav
  > a:hover::before {
  color: var(--brand);
}

input[type="text"] {
  border: 1px solid;
}

@media
  (max-width: 600px) and
  (width >= 40.5em) {
  .button {
    color: blue;
  }
  @supports (display: grid) {
    .grid {
      display: grid;
    }
  }
}

@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter";
}

@KEYFRAMES spin {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}

@scope (.card) to (.content) {
  img {
    border: 0;
  }
}

.card {
  &:hover {
    color: blue;
  }
}
